        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Old Church Slavonic</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 863 CE – c. 1100 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Family</span><span class="info-stat-val">Slavic; South Slavic branch, Eastern South Slavic subgroup; thought to be based mainly on the 9th-century Slavic dialect of the Byzantine theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Writing</span><span class="info-stat-val">Glagolitic, developed for the Moravian mission in 862/863 (about two-thirds of the canon); Cyrillic, developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> Literary School and official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 893</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Earliest records</span><span class="info-stat-val">the translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> from 862/863 (the Aprakos Evangeliar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>); the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is usually thought to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> Missal, of the late 10th century; most manuscripts date from the late 10th and early 11th centuries</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Status today</span><span class="info-stat-val">extinct as such; by the mid-11th century it had grown into the regional recensions of Church Slavonic, still the liturgical language of the Bulgarian, Russian, Serbian, Ukrainian and Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches and of several Eastern Catholic churches</span></div>
