    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Old Church Slavonic was shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> for their mission to Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> from 863, with a new alphabet, Glagolitic, and translations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and liturgy for the Christianisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>.</li><li>Expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, their students carried it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, where Boris I founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> schools, Cyrillic was developed and the language became the language of state and church in 893.</li><li>As the oldest attested Slavic language it preserves features of Proto-Slavic, and as Church Slavonic it held among the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> a place like that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The full text of the Second Book of Enoch survives only in Old Church Slavonic, although the original had almost certainly been written in Greek or even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

